#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105380 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "cdad8b80-f85f-5555-92da-58a974f322a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12380" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n$\\iint\\limits_{S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^2}} \\: \\textrm{d}S$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z - x + y^{2}}}dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(1)/(\\sqrt{(1 + x + y^{2}) - x + y^{2}}) \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 1^{2} + (2y)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2 + 4y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2(1 + 2y^{2})}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{2}dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (1 - 0) \\cdot (1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105381 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "558bc791-1cd4-51fc-9e2c-9efdd063323d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12381" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S$,\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1264/Source/Images/8fda75933a423952bc1fd00fcda06419.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)x^{2}ydS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{2}y \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} + (-1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{2}y \\cdot \\sqrt{4}dA \\\\\n&= 2∬_(D)x^{2}ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∬_(D)x^{2}ydA &= 2∬_(Δ)f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∬_(Δ)r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ \\cdot r\\sin θ \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∬_(Δ)r^{4}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θ∫_{0}^{1}r^{4}drdθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θ[\\frac{r^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{15}[- \\cos^{3} θ]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{15}(1 + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105382 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "02451ec6-38a3-5cfc-adfb-912b91fab9dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12382" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz$, where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$.\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below.\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (2x)^{2} + (-1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x(x^{2} - y)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - xy)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d3b845e-10e3-59ef-9b6a-f7b4ccc07856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102182} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb49055-7fe5-5c17-8ed4-03e806972011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102183} {:answer/id #uuid "4938439a-6791-521b-95db-38906acf32e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102184} {:answer/id #uuid "6b83a001-1281-5cd5-8a65-2e4d495f3df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102185} {:answer/id #uuid "7d93202d-56bc-5ec5-b992-287e5234398d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4938439a-6791-521b-95db-38906acf32e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102184} :answer-field/id #uuid "6277bd1b-5049-58be-8ac3-6ed18b165039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102181}] :question/id #uuid "1a32ff2f-f5fc-57a4-9ce0-9a59d7c231d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161355" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$∬_(S)(z)/(\\sqrt{1 + 2\\cos^{2} (x + y)})dS$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = \\sin (x + y)$ for $0 \\le x \\le π$ and $-x \\le y \\le x$."}]}
